  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this afternoon exposes a center of high \npressure over central Lake Michigan. This high is responsible \nfor the dry weather and largely sunny skies today. It'll keep \nconditions quiet through tomorrow before ejecting to the east \nclearing the way for our next rain chances. Another seasonably \nwarm day is expected tomorrow with highs forecast in the lower \nand middle 80s. Tomorrow will again be mostly sunny, but high \nlevel clouds will start moving over later in the day ahead of \nthis next storm system.\n\nA midlevel shortwave disturbance is churning some showers over \nthe Intermountain West this afternoon. Over the next 36 hours or\nso, this wave will sharpen up considerably, evolving into \nclosed mid level low by early Friday as it works into the \nMidwest. Widespread showers are expected to blossom around the \ndeepening surface low over the northern Plains tomorrow and \ntrack southeastward into our general vicinity on Friday. There \nis fair agreement on the onset of rain in our west being late \nmorning to early afternoon, and into Chicago during the \nafternoon hours. Widespread rain is then favored around the CWA \nFriday evening into early Saturday. There remains some \nuncertainty in trends through the rest of Saturday, but coverage\nshould wane through the day generally from north to south. Most\nof the area looks to be dry by Saturday evening, maybe a bit \nearlier across much of Chicagoland. Where global guidance had \npreviously had this closed low becoming cutoff and meandering \nabout the lower Midwest early next week, now the favored signal \nis for the system to get sheared off to the northeast on Sunday.\nWhile a few lingering showers still appear possible on the \nbackside of the low, mainly around the southern/southeastern \nCWA, precip potential for Sunday is on a downward trend.\n\nStill not expecting much in the way of embedded thunder \ncoverage with such cool low level air greatly limiting \ninstability through the column. Some embedded storms will be \npossible, certainly, with some instability around and a strong \nforcing. There's some uncertainty regarding the track of the low\nand, while its still favored to track south of the CWA, a \nfarther north track is still on the table. The NAM, UKMET, and \nRRFS are all farther north with the track and, perhaps more \nnotably, the position of the effective warm front and northern \nextent of the more supportive instability. This is due to these \ncamps resolving a deeper, more tightly wound upper low. If such \na solution were to verify, this would boost embedded storm \nchances for areas that end up south of the warm front, though \nstill not seeing a signal for anything too strong or severe in \nthat case. There is concern, however, from a heavy rain \nperspective. Deep saturation with seasonably high PWATs just \nunder 2\" in conjunction with deep, dynamic forcing could result \nin periods of heavy, soaking rainfall. A widespread 1\"+ of QPF \nthrough Saturday has been a consistent signal among \ndeterministic camps and WPC has the area in a slight risk (2 of \n4) for excessive rainfall.\n\nConditions will be slightly cooler this weekend with the storm \nnearby. Daily highs are forecast in the middle 70s to lower 80s,\nlikely coolest all around on Saturday. Gusty northerly winds \nmay develop on Sunday, especially down the lake, as a high \nencroaches from the north which may produce choppy waves and \ndangerous swimming conditions. A steady warmup is progged for \nnext week with largely quiet conditions favored through the \nearly part of the week.\n\nDoom",
